      "content": "I understand you're looking for an immediate relief method; however, using milk or any milk substitute is not advisable for chemical burns to your eye. Milk is not a recommended treatment for eye exposure to chemicals as it can cause additional complications, including infection or further irritation. Additionally, the contact with milk may alter the skin's pH around the eye and potentially delay proper healing. In the case of chemical exposure, rinsing with clean, cool water is the best immediate action to take; it helps to dilute and possibly flush out any remaining chemicals. After thorough rinsing, continue with gentle cleaning of the eye area. While chamomile or other similar anti-inflammatory eye drops can sometimes provide relief, they may still not directly neutralize the chemicals. An eye burn due to a harsh degreaser is a serious symptom and could potentially lead to more severe issues if not appropriately treated. If burning and stinging signs continue, even after flushing and cooling, it's important to seek professional medical help to rule out any significant damage or consequent infections. As a rule, always err on the side of caution with eye safety.",
